WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.540
Speaker 1: Let's go to Grant from Iowa City,
Iowa.

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:03.759
Grant, welcome to the program.

00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:04.759
What's on your mind today?

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:10.580
Speaker 3: Hey, David, I saw your interview
with Ben Dixon yesterday.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:11.599
It was interesting.

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:13.340
Thank you.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.020
It's clear that the Christian nationalist
movement is definitely growing or at least

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.760
becoming more bold.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.990
Yes.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:24.680
And so I was just wondering I know I don't
know if you identify as like agnostic or atheist,

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:25.680
I'm not sure.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.180
But so I don't know how much interaction like
personally you've had with evangelical conservative

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:32.250
Christians.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:38.360
But do you think not the politicians, but
like just like general people in public, you

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:44.550
think they're motivated more by a desire for
power, or are they motivated more by fear?

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:50.020
Personally, in my experience, I kind of grew
up in that kind of, you know, realm.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.329
And I think there's a there's a great fear
they have obviously afraid of like God punishing

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:55.329
them.

00:00:55.329 --> 00:01:01.239
Speaker 1: So I my my most extensive interactions
with American evangelicals was when I went

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:05.149
to Indiana because my girlfriend has family
there.

00:01:05.149 --> 00:01:07.600
And the my my girlfriend's Jewish.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.960
But is she has a part of her family that's
evangelical Christians.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:20.270
And what I saw in speaking to them, a lot
of fear and a lot of fear driven, conspiratorial

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.210
beliefs.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.079
And it did not feel to me like.

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:25.430
Now I'm not talking about politicians.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:26.819
I'm talking about random people.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:27.819
Which was your question?

00:01:27.819 --> 00:01:30.520
It did not really a thirst for power.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:31.640
Was there a guiding light?

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.840
It genuinely seemed like it was fear.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.189
Speaker 3: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:39.409
It's just interesting to me because I think.

00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:43.490
Just in my own experience, like when I went
to college and stuff and just, you know, classes

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.600
were for looking at other groups in the world.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.539
And I was like, Oh, this is not that scary.

00:01:50.539 --> 00:01:51.539
And.

00:01:51.539 --> 00:01:52.659
There's nothing to be afraid of.

00:01:52.659 --> 00:01:58.819
And, like, I don't know, even just like, I'm
still Christian, but like my view of, like,

00:01:58.819 --> 00:02:00.380
God has changed so much from what it used
to be.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:04.529
It just like this was angry God who wanted
to punish us any time he could.

00:02:04.529 --> 00:02:09.420
So I know I kind of think I've enjoyed talking
with more conservative Christians and evangelicals

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.150
over the last few years because I kind of
like just get to know how they're feeling

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:17.000
about stuff and and perspectives.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.450
And for some of them it seems like, Oh, okay,
they start to learn like it's it's not as,

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.890
you know, horrifying as it might be, I guess,
to.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:26.890
Yeah.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.010
Speaker 1: That's really interesting to hear
in that you came to that conclusion because

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.390
one of the things that I've thought about
is, you know, I guess I guess I consider myself

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.799
agnostic, although whenever I explain my views,
people will write in and they'll say, David,

00:02:35.799 --> 00:02:37.829
you're actually an atheist, whatever.

00:02:37.829 --> 00:02:38.829
Call me whatever you want.

00:02:38.829 --> 00:02:44.620
But my view is I think it's completely possible
that some kind of God or higher power exists.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.420
I've just not seen any evidence of it.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:53.090
But one of the things that I've come to believe
is contrary to much of this fire and brimstone

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:59.200
stuff from the evangelical side, if there
were such a higher power, it seems to me that

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:07.950
if God created such complicated human beings,
God would not be this black, white, vindictive

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:12.709
arbiter that some Christianity makes him out
to be.

00:03:12.709 --> 00:03:15.150
And it sounds sort of like you've come to
the same idea.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.349
Speaker 3: Oh, yeah, absolutely.

00:03:17.349 --> 00:03:18.349
Absolutely.

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:21.530
So, well, I can let you get some other people
now.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:22.530
All right.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:23.530
But I just thank you so much.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:24.530
Speaker 1: I appreciate the call.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:25.530
Speaker 3: Yeah, thanks for taking my call.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:29.629
Speaker 1: And in fact, what Grant from Iowa
is letting me do is just end the segment because

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:31.310
we are at the end of our time today.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:39.000
I always am just so broken up that I cannot
get to everyone who's waiting to talk to me.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.430
But that means that hopefully next week some
people will call in.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:43.780
So I will speak to you then.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.779
We'll take a quick break and be back with
more right after this.

